# Vite logs files
vite.config.js.timestamp-*
vite.config.ts.timestamp-*

# Ride-log encryption. The private key is the ONLY thing that can read the
# sealed logs — it must never leave the laptop, and never reach the repo or the
# Pi. The public key is cryptographically safe to publish, but it is specific to
# one laptop and this is a public repo, so keep both out.
*.private.pem
*.public.pem
ride-log-key.private.pem
# No trailing slash, same reason as node_modules: sealed logs are big enough to
# want to park on an external disk and symlink back.
ride-logs
*.celog

# VCU parameter snapshots written by service mode (src/vcu/sweep.ts). Generated output,
# and specific to one motorcycle — the name table it is read against IS committed
# (src/vcu/param-table.ts), so another owner gets everything they need to take their
# own. Committing one deliberately, as a reference set of real Eva Ribelle values,
# would be a fine thing to do; it just should not happen by accident on every run.
vcu-params

# Built by scripts/build-service-preview.ts — regenerate rather than commit
service-sheet-preview.html

# Local ride database and scratch probes — never commit (large, and full of VIN/GPS)
rides.db
rides.db-shm
rides.db-wal
rides.db.bak-*
energica_probe.py

# Screenshots and the built previews behind them, written by scripts/build-service-preview.ts and
# taken through chrome-devtools for the dashboard gate. Generated, large, and regenerated per
# review — and an untracked directory of .html is exactly the kind of local-only state that made a
# green worktree disagree with CI once already.
preview-shots/

# Ride logs are large encrypted blobs and are never committed — decrypt them on the Mac with
# scripts/decrypt-log.ts. The small text extracts beside them ARE tracked: docs/charge-auto.md and
# docs/can-0x121-charge-command.md cite them as the provenance for #186's measurements.
evidence/*.celog
# Trailing glob rather than `evidence/*.db`: a scratch DB opened read-write leaves `-shm`
# and `-wal` siblings next to a file that was 1.98 GB here. `rides.db-shm`/`-wal` below are
# enumerated one by one for that same reason; a glob covers the case without the list.
evidence/*.db*

# Bulk material pulled off the Pi for one investigation. Same rule as the ride logs
# above: the raw thing is never committed and the DOC carries the extract — #214's
# docs/vcu-reverse-and-backup.md quotes the audit record and the probe values it cites.
# evidence/captures/ reached 115 MB of candump text in one night, which `git add -A`
# will happily sweep into a public repo if nothing stops it.
evidence/captures/
evidence/service-writes.jsonl
evidence/probes-*.txt

# Recovered waypoints carry coordinates; this is a PUBLIC repo and docs/route-map.md
# forbids them in committed files. scripts/recover-waypoints.ts writes outside the repo
# by default, and this is the second line of defence for a --gpx that points inside it.
*.gpx

# Scratch databases decrypted from a .celog for one investigation — `scripts/decrypt-log.ts
# --out evidence/scratch-<date>.db`. A full /dl dump rebuilds to ~2 GB of VIN, GPS and ride
# history, and `evidence/` is a TRACKED directory, so without this the file sits one
# `git add -A` away from a public repo. The -shm/-wal siblings are matched by the same glob
# because SQLite in WAL mode leaves them behind whenever a reader exits uncleanly.
evidence/*.db
evidence/*.db-shm
evidence/*.db-wal

# Rendered route-map screenshots show exactly where the bike went — the same thing the
# coordinate rule forbids in text, in a form that is harder to notice. Keep them local and
# hand them over out of band; a map PNG in a public repo is a published GPS trace.
evidence/*.png
evidence/*.jpg
